WEBVTT

00:00:11.870 --> 00:00:24.800
<v Ira Glass>Back in June, Rick Cole was sitting in a meeting of the Pasadena Municipal Services Committee in City Hall there. That's about as dull a sentence as you could start a podcast with. But then things happen. Rick's a council member.
<v Ira Glass>早在六月，里克·科尔坐在帕萨迪纳市政厅市政服务委员会的会议上。这大概是你开始播客时能用的最枯燥的一句话了。但随后事情发生了。里克是一位市议员。

00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:48.920
<v Rick Cole>And I get a text that I glance at from the police chief to the city council that says, "At 5:30, we're making a public announcement that there will be military exercises in Northeast Pasadena. They will take place at St. Luke Hospital, but do not reveal the location." This is at 5:24, six minutes before the public is to be informed.
<v Rick Cole>我收到了一条短信，我瞥了一眼，是警察局长发给市议会的，上面写着：“下午5点半，我们将发布公告，称帕萨迪纳东北部将进行军事演习。演习将在圣卢克医院进行，但不要透露具体地点。”当时是下午5点24分，距离公众被告知还有六分钟。

00:00:48.920 --> 00:00:51.720
<v Ira Glass>So you saw that and you thought what?
<v Ira Glass>所以你看到了那个，你当时想的是什么？

00:00:51.720 --> 00:00:56.000
<v Rick Cole>Well, I thought, that's really strange.
<v Rick Cole>嗯，我想，这真的很奇怪。

00:00:56.000 --> 00:01:09.000
<v Ira Glass>It's not unprecedented there would be military exercises in a residential area. That happens in America. But still, it's strange when it's your area, with no notice at all. The meeting ends. Rick runs to another event.
<v Ira Glass>在居民区进行军事演习并非史无前例。这在美国发生过。但即使这样，当这发生在你自己的辖区，而且完全没有提前通知时，还是很奇怪。会议结束了。里克跑去参加另一个活动。

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:28.320
<v Rick Cole>Meanwhile, I had told my assistant to post on our Instagram account that this military exercise was happening and to actually identify the location. And she told me, you know, they told us very sternly not to say the location. And I said, the people deserve to know where this is happening.
<v Rick Cole>与此同时，我告诉我的助理在我们的Instagram账号上发布这个军事演习正在进行的消息，并指明具体地点。她告诉我，你知道，他们非常严肃地告诉我们不要透露地点。我说，人民有权知道这在哪里发生。

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:30.920
<v Ira Glass>And did she post the name of the hospital?
<v Ira Glass>她发布了医院的名字吗？

00:01:30.920 --> 00:01:33.040
<v Rick Cole>She did.
<v Rick Cole>她发布了。

00:01:33.040 --> 00:01:37.280
<v Ira Glass>Because you just figured, what are they going to do to me? I'm just going to do this?
<v Ira Glass>因为你只是想，他们能拿我怎么样？我就是要这么做？

00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:40.360
<v Rick Cole>Exactly. I mean, that's the way I've lived my life, Ira.
<v Rick Cole>没错。我的意思是，艾拉，这就是我一直以来的生活方式。

00:01:40.360 --> 00:02:09.690
<v Ira Glass>He's got stories. If you ever run into Rick, get him to tell you the one about how he tried to stop Christopher Columbus's great great, great great great, great great great great great, great great great great great, great great great grandson from becoming grand marshal of Pasadena's Rose Parade. But I digress. Anyway, that night in June, he heads over to this hospital, a creepy old building with broken windows and peeling paint. It's been abandoned for years. And, sure enough, sometime around 8:00 PM--
<v Ira Glass>他有很多故事。如果你有机会遇到里克，一定要让他讲讲他是如何阻止克里斯托弗·哥伦布的第N代孙子成为帕萨迪纳玫瑰花车游行大礼官的故事。但我扯远了。总之，那个六月的夜晚，他前往那家医院，那是一栋破旧的建筑，窗户破碎，油漆剥落。它已经被废弃多年了。果然，在大约晚上8点左右——

00:02:09.690 --> 00:02:27.410
<v Rick Cole>These giant helicopters can be heard in the distance, getting closer and closer. Then they disgorge paratroopers clambering down ropes onto the roof of the hospital. Flashbang grenades are going off. Simulated gunfire is rattling through the neighborhood. I mean, it was jaw dropping.
<v Rick Cole>远处可以听到巨大的直升机声，越来越近。然后它们放下伞兵，顺着绳子爬上医院屋顶。闪光弹爆炸了。模拟枪声在社区里回荡。我的意思是，这简直令人震惊。

00:02:27.410 --> 00:02:54.450
<v Ira Glass>This is all supposed to end by 8:30, but in fact, it wasn't over until about 2:00 AM. To Rick, this was outrageous. Why do this in a residential neighborhood with no warning, so people had no chance to prepare, maybe go elsewhere for the night, maybe give their pets tranquilizers so they don't freak out? Now people were hearing all the commotion, probably going onto their phones to figure out what was happening. And he thought he needed to jump in and explain.
<v Ira Glass>这一切本应在8点半结束，但实际上直到凌晨2点左右才结束。对里克来说，这太离谱了。为什么要在没有预警的情况下在居民区这样做，让人们没有机会准备，也许晚上可以去别处，或者给宠物服用镇静剂以免它们受惊？现在人们听到了所有的骚动，可能正在用手机查看发生了什么。他认为他必须介入并解释。

00:02:54.450 --> 00:03:00.550
<v Rick Cole>Councilmember Rick Cole here in front of the former St. Luke's Hospital. As you can hear--
<v Rick Cole>市议员里克·科尔在圣卢克医院旧址前。正如你所听到的——

00:03:00.550 --> 00:03:06.130
<v Ira Glass>He's in the dark. Explosions are flashing behind him, like he's some sort of suburban war correspondent.
<v Ira Glass>他在黑暗中。爆炸在他身后闪烁，就像他是某种郊区战地记者一样。

00:03:06.130 --> 00:03:12.780
<v Rick Cole>Flashbangs and simulated gunfire going on-- they're going to be going on for the next half an hour, we're told.
<v Rick Cole>据我们所知，闪光弹和模拟枪声还要持续半小时。

00:03:12.780 --> 00:03:28.690
<v Rick Cole>I was focused on the fact that I should tell people what's going on, because their dogs are going to bark. Their kids are going to ask questions. And they're going to wonder, why are helicopters descending on Pasadena? Why are they hearing gunfire in the neighborhood?
<v Rick Cole>我专注于我应该告诉人们发生了什么，因为他们的狗会叫。他们的孩子会问问题。他们会想，为什么直升机会降落在帕萨迪纳？为什么他们在社区里听到枪声？

00:03:28.690 --> 00:03:37.950
<v Ira Glass>He'd done videos like this in the past, in the wake of the Eaton Fire last year, letting people know when it was safe to drink the water or eat vegetables from their gardens, that kind of thing.
<v Ira Glass>他过去曾做过这样的视频，在去年的伊顿大火后，让人们知道什么时候可以安全饮用水或食用花园里的蔬菜，这类事情。

00:03:37.950 --> 00:03:45.630
<v Rick Cole>And this is going to go on for a while. And good luck to the people that have got to get up and go to work tomorrow morning.
<v Rick Cole>而且这种情况还会持续一段时间。祝那些明天早上还要起床去上班的人好运吧。

00:03:45.630 --> 00:03:46.890
<v Rick Cole>[GUNFIRE]
<v Rick Cole>[枪声]

00:03:46.890 --> 00:03:53.710
<v Ira Glass>It's funny. The weird thing about this is that it seems like you all were supposed to act like this was not a weird thing to happen.
<v Ira Glass>很有趣。这件事最奇怪的地方在于，似乎你们所有人都应该表现得好像这并不是什么奇怪的事情。

00:03:53.710 --> 00:03:54.930
<v Rick Cole>That's exactly.
<v Rick Cole>确实如此。

00:03:54.930 --> 00:03:59.580
<v Ira Glass>Did it make you feel sort of crazy to be told, like, oh, no, this is normal, don't sweat it?
<v Ira Glass>有人告诉你“哦，不，这是正常的，别担心”，这让你觉得有点疯狂吗？

00:03:59.580 --> 00:04:22.860
<v Rick Cole>[LAUGHS] I wouldn't say crazy. It infuriated me. We owe, to the people we serve, to treat them with respect and to keep them informed about things that affect them. And this was such a egregious breach of that trust.
<v Rick Cole>[笑] 我不会说疯狂。它让我非常愤怒。我们亏欠我们服务的人民，要尊重他们，并让他们了解影响他们的事情。而这完全是对这种信任的严重背叛。

00:04:22.860 --> 00:04:42.340
<v Ira Glass>Rick couldn't help but notice that nobody in the military seemed to care how everyday citizens were experiencing all this. And as a 73-year-old councilmember, he felt a duty to take what he saw as a more old-fashioned approach and reached, by the way, nearly 8 million people with his videos informing the public, succeeding more than he ever imagined.
<v Ira Glass>里克不禁注意到，军方似乎没人关心普通市民是如何经历这一切的。作为一名73岁的议员，他感到有责任采取他所认为更老派的方法。顺便说一句，他的视频通过告知公众，触及了近800万人，取得了超乎他想象的成功。

00:04:42.340 --> 00:05:04.340
<v Ira Glass>Today on our program, we have stories of people who see a future arrive that feels uglier and meaner than the past. And it's not like they can turn that around. It's way too big. But in every story today, there is somebody who takes a stand and tries to clean up the mess that's around them or call attention to that mess and say no.
<v Ira Glass>今天在我们的节目中，我们有关于人们目睹未来的故事，那个未来感觉比过去更丑陋、更刻薄。他们无法扭转局面。它太庞大了。但在今天的每个故事中，都有人挺身而出，试图清理他们周围的混乱，或者呼吁人们关注这种混乱并说不。

00:05:04.340 --> 00:05:30.660
<v Ira Glass>We have stories today of some very stubborn and idealistic people, from a small town in Maine to a school in Russia. From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>今天我们有一些非常固执和理想主义者的故事，从缅因州的一个小镇到俄罗斯的一所学校。来自芝加哥WBEZ，这里是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。请继续收听。

00:05:30.660 --> 00:06:09.060
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life, Act One, Closing Time. Sometimes, as things change in a country because of big historic forces, doctors get put in some weird and difficult situations. That's what happened in this first act in Maine, way up north in Aroostook County. That's so far north that the Canadian border is closer than the nearest US city. The local hospital there is Houlton Regional Hospital. And last year, they announced they were going to close their labor and delivery unit, with one month's notice. Even the unit's nurse manager, Heather Quint, had no idea this was actually coming.
<v Ira Glass>这里是《美国生活》，第一幕，打烊时间。有时，随着一个国家因重大的历史力量而发生变化，医生们会被置于一些奇怪且困难的境地。这就是在缅因州北部阿鲁斯图克县发生的第一幕故事。那里北到加拿大边境比最近的美国城市还要近。当地的医院是霍尔顿地区医院。去年，他们宣布将关闭分娩中心，提前一个月通知。甚至该部门的护士长希瑟·昆特也不知道这真的要发生。

00:06:09.060 --> 00:06:33.400
<v Heather Quint>It was delivered very abruptly and matter of factly, I guess. This is what's happening. Our birth rates are going down. We can't recruit. It's not, basically, a money-maker. So we're closing. There was no, I'm sorry. We hate to-- it was just very matter of factly.
<v Heather Quint>我想，这个消息传达得非常突兀且公事公办。这就是正在发生的事。我们的出生率在下降。我们招不到人。基本上，它不是一个盈利部门。所以我们要关闭了。没有一句“我很抱歉”。我们很讨厌……这只是非常公事公办。

00:06:33.400 --> 00:06:53.590
<v Ira Glass>It is pretty matter-of-fact reality at this point in America, rural hospitals closing. But labor and delivery departments in particular, they've been vanishing in lots of rural areas. And when a community loses a place to have babies, in the first weeks after that happens, that's when you really see what it means in people's lives.
<v Ira Glass>目前在美国，乡村医院关闭是一个非常客观的现实。但尤其是分娩部门，在许多农村地区正在消失。当一个社区失去了一个生孩子的地方，在最初几周后，你才会真正看到这对人们的生活意味着什么。

00:06:53.590 --> 00:07:20.430
<v Ira Glass>And the story in Maine unfolded just after Houlton Regional Hospital packed up its labor and delivery department, closed the doors there. As that was happening, a woman who lives in the area, named Katie Gowell, was pregnant with her fifth child. This was a low-risk pregnancy. She was planning to give birth at home, just like she'd done with her fourth child. Her family doctor, who was a midwife before she became a doctor, was going to deliver the baby. So that was the plan.
<v Ira Glass>缅因州的故事就发生在霍尔顿地区医院收拾好分娩部门并关闭大门之后。就在这时，住在该地区的一位名叫凯蒂·高威尔的女性怀上了第五个孩子。这是一次低风险的怀孕。她计划像生第四个孩子那样在家分娩。她在成为医生前曾是助产士的家庭医生将为她接生。这就是计划。

00:07:20.430 --> 00:07:51.670
<v Ira Glass>29 days after Houlton closed its labor and delivery department, Katie Gowell went into labor at home. Reporter Elaine Appleton Grant has the story of what happened next. Quick heads up before we start-- some of us here on staff found ourselves thinking about the TV show The Pitt as we were making this story, because there's no way to explain what happened without diving into some very vivid medical details during childbirth. I just want to say, if that's not for you, please know that is part of this story. Here's Elaine Appleton Grant.
<v Ira Glass>霍尔顿关闭分娩部门29天后，凯蒂·高威尔在家中开始分娩。记者伊莱恩·阿普尔顿·格兰特讲述了接下来的故事。在开始之前先提醒一下——我们在制作这个故事时，我们团队中的一些人想到了电视节目《急诊室的故事》，因为如果不深入探讨分娩过程中一些非常生动的医疗细节，就无法解释发生了什么。我只想说，如果这不适合你，请知道这是故事的一部分。这里是伊莱恩·阿普尔顿·格兰特。

00:07:51.670 --> 00:08:14.730
<v Ira Glass>(HOST) ELAINE APPLETON
<v Ira Glass>（主持人）伊莱恩·阿普尔顿

00:08:14.730 --> 00:08:23.110
<v Katie Gowell>I'm like, well, Mom, maybe I shouldn't have called them already, because it was sort of-- I kind of went like a standstill for a while.
<v Katie Gowell>我说，嗯，妈妈，也许我不应该已经给他们打电话了，因为有一段时间它有点停滞了。

00:08:23.110 --> 00:08:30.150
<v Katie Gowell>(HOST) ELAINE APPLETON
<v Katie Gowell>（主持人）伊莱恩·阿普尔顿

00:08:30.150 --> 00:08:44.470
<v Rose Fuchs>But all of a sudden, it was like someone poked me awake. And I sat bolt upright. And I said, what did Katie say? And Katie was down the hallway in the bathroom at that point. So I went down there, and I said, did you say something? And she said, yeah. I said, my water broke.
<v Rose Fuchs>但突然之间，就像有人把我戳醒了。我坐得笔直。我说，凯蒂说了什么？那时候凯蒂在走廊尽头的浴室里。所以我走过去，我说，你说了什么吗？她说，是的。我说，我的羊水破了。

00:08:44.470 --> 00:08:47.630
<v Rose Fuchs>(HOST) ELAINE APPLETON
<v Rose Fuchs>（主持人）伊莱恩·阿普尔顿

00:08:47.630 --> 00:08:56.040
<v Katie Gowell>And then I just sat there for a few minutes. And so then I'm like, well, I'm going to get up now. And then I went to wipe, and I felt something.
<v Katie Gowell>然后我就坐在那里几分钟。然后我说，好吧，我现在要起床了。然后我去擦拭时，感觉到了一些东西。

00:08:56.040 --> 00:09:20.200
<v Rose Fuchs>And she said, what's that? And I looked down in the toilet, and there was probably 8 inches of cord hanging out, not quite touching the toilet water, but close. So I was like, immediately, this is very bad. This is a prolapsed cord, which rarely happens. I just said, it looks like the cord. We need to check you out. And let's get in the bed.
<v Rose Fuchs>她说，那是什么？我低头看马桶，大概有8英寸长的脐带垂出来，没有完全触碰到马桶水，但很接近。我立刻觉得，这非常糟糕。这是一个脐带脱垂，很少发生。我只是说，看起来是脐带。我们需要检查一下。让我们上床。

00:09:20.200 --> 00:09:55.820
<v Rose Fuchs>(HOST) ELAINE APPLETON
<v Rose Fuchs>（主持人）伊莱恩·阿普尔顿

00:09:55.820 --> 00:10:13.840
<v Rose Fuchs>And we got her on the bed. And I was checking to see if she's completely dilated, ready to push the baby out. I thought, it's her fifth baby. I thought, if we could give one push and finish this, I'll do that. But that wasn't the case. She was probably about 6 centimeters.
<v Rose Fuchs>我们让她躺在床上。我正在检查她是否完全扩张，准备把孩子生出来。我想，这是她的第五个孩子。我想，如果我们能用力推一下并完成它，我会这么做。但事实并非如此。她大概开了6厘米。

00:10:13.840 --> 00:11:12.380
<v Rose Fuchs>(HOST) ELAINE APPLETON
<v Rose Fuchs>（主持人）伊莱恩·阿普尔顿

00:11:12.380 --> 00:11:19.040
<v Rose Fuchs>I was terrified, because I didn't want the baby to die.
<v Rose Fuchs>我吓坏了，因为我不想让孩子死。

00:11:19.040 --> 00:11:21.720
<v Rose Fuchs>(INTERVIEWER) ELAINE APPLETON
<v Rose Fuchs>（采访者）伊莱恩·阿普尔顿

00:11:21.720 --> 00:11:44.360
<v Rose Fuchs>I turned around. And my nurse has beautiful big, blue eyes. And they always comfort me in the middle of anything. And I turned around, and I looked in her eyes. And I said, call 911. And her eyes went two sizes bigger because she's actually never heard me say that in working with her 12 years. Yeah.
<v Rose Fuchs>我转过身。我的护士有一双漂亮的大蓝眼睛。在任何情况下，它们总是能安慰我。我转过身，看着她的眼睛。我说，打911。她的眼睛瞪大了两倍，因为在她和我共事12年里，从来没听过我说这句话。是的。

00:11:44.360 --> 00:11:46.580
<v Rose Fuchs>(HOST) ELAINE APPLETON
<v Rose Fuchs>（主持人）伊莱恩·阿普尔顿

00:11:46.580 --> 00:11:57.530
<v Rose Fuchs>I started to coach her that the life of the baby is at stake, and you're going to have to go through quite a bit if we're going to hope to save your child.
<v Rose Fuchs>我开始指导她，孩子的生命危在旦夕，如果你想救你的孩子，你必须经历很多痛苦。

00:11:57.530 --> 00:12:05.170
<v Rose Fuchs>(HOST) ELAINE APPLETON
<v Rose Fuchs>（主持人）伊莱恩·阿普尔顿

00:12:05.170 --> 00:12:19.370
<v Katie Gowell>Because me, being in the moment, I don't remember every detail, where she would remember every detail. And I trust her with telling my story. I trust her completely with everything.
<v Katie Gowell>因为我当时身处其中，我不记得每一个细节，而她记得每一个细节。我信任她讲述我的故事。我完全信任她处理一切。

00:12:19.370 --> 00:13:03.130
<v Katie Gowell>(HOST) ELAINE APPLETON
<v Katie Gowell>（主持人）伊莱恩·阿普尔顿

00:13:03.130 --> 00:13:24.370
<v Rose Fuchs>I chose what I thought was the best course of action, which-- I gently replaced it into the vagina. Is that OK to say on tape? OK, gently replaced it inside so that the warmth of her body would keep the cord warm, because I knew we're in Maine, and although it was June, it wasn't that warm.
<v Rose Fuchs>我选择了我认为最好的行动方案，那就是——我轻轻地把它放回阴道里。在录音带上说这个没问题吧？好的，轻轻地放进去，这样她身体的温度可以保持脐带温暖，因为我知道我们在缅因州，虽然是六月，但并不那么暖和。

00:13:24.370 --> 00:13:46.440
<v Rose Fuchs>(HOST) ELAINE APPLETON
<v Rose Fuchs>（主持人）伊莱恩·阿普尔顿

00:13:46.440 --> 00:13:55.160
<v Rose Fuchs>I shifted myself and climbed onto the bed between her legs, crouching, sort of on all fours, kneeling, crouching down.
<v Rose Fuchs>我挪动了一下，爬上床，双腿分开，蹲着，四肢着地，跪着，蹲伏下来。

00:13:55.160 --> 00:14:01.940
<v Rose Fuchs>(HOST) ELAINE APPLETON
<v Rose Fuchs>（主持人）伊莱恩·阿普尔顿

00:14:01.940 --> 00:14:28.260
<v Rose Fuchs>I was internally inside the patient, pushing upward toward her head as, basically, hard as I could, using two fingers to push up, the rest of my fingers to lightly be touching the cord to keep track of the heart tones at all times. Because if they slowed down, I knew something's pinching. If they slowed down, I also knew I needed to push up higher and get the head up higher.
<v Rose Fuchs>我在患者体内，尽量向上朝着她的头推，基本上是用尽全力，用两根手指推，其余手指轻轻触碰脐带，时刻追踪心音。因为如果它们慢下来，我就知道有什么东西在挤压。如果它们慢下来，我也知道我需要推得更高，把头顶得更高。

00:14:28.260 --> 00:14:33.440
<v Rose Fuchs>(INTERVIEWER) ELAINE APPLETON
<v Rose Fuchs>（采访者）伊莱恩·阿普尔顿

00:14:33.440 --> 00:15:20.740
<v Rose Fuchs>Probably up to my wrist, starting forearm. Meanwhile, Katie's own body wants to push the baby out, right? Contractions push down. So to have my hand, that's painful for her. And I knew it was painful. And I told her, I'm very sorry, but I can't care about your pain. It's a very weird pain. It's a deep pain. Anyone who's had a cervical biopsy, an endometrial biopsy, an IUD put in-- I think it's a pain no one can describe. And then this is times a whole lot more, because there's a baby in there too, and now someone's hand as well.
<v Rose Fuchs>大概到我的手腕，开始到前臂。与此同时，凯蒂的身体想要把孩子推出来，对吧？宫缩向下推。所以我的手在里面，这让她很痛苦。我知道这很痛苦。我告诉她，我非常抱歉，但我现在无法顾及你的疼痛。这是一种非常奇怪的疼痛。这是一种深层的疼痛。任何做过宫颈活检、子宫内膜活检、放过节育环的人——我想这是一种无法描述的痛。而这还要严重得多，因为里面还有一个婴儿，现在还有一只手。

00:15:20.740 --> 00:15:54.410
<v Rose Fuchs>(HOST) ELAINE APPLETON
<v Rose Fuchs>（主持人）伊莱恩·阿普尔顿

00:15:54.410 --> 00:16:04.430
<v Rose Fuchs>I said, no, we're not. And they said, well, that's what they're saying. I said, I don't care what they're saying. We are not doing that. You tell them a doctor's on site.
<v Rose Fuchs>我说，不，我们不去。他们说，嗯，这就是他们说的。我说，我不在乎他们怎么说。我们不会那样做。你告诉他们有医生在现场。

00:16:04.430 --> 00:17:46.050
<v Rose Fuchs>(HOST) ELAINE APPLETON
<v Rose Fuchs>（主持人）伊莱恩·阿普尔顿

00:17:46.050 --> 00:17:53.510
<v Rose Fuchs>And I said, you call Houlton Regional. You're telling them we're coming. And I mean, we're coming.
<v Rose Fuchs>我说，你打给霍尔顿地区医院。你告诉他们我们要去了。我的意思是，我们要去了。

00:17:53.510 --> 00:18:24.550
<v Rose Fuchs>(HOST) ELAINE APPLETON
<v Rose Fuchs>（主持人）伊莱恩·阿普尔顿

00:18:24.550 --> 00:18:28.290
<v Rose Fuchs>I said, Sasha, then get a chainsaw and cut the wall down.
<v Rose Fuchs>我说，萨沙，那就拿把电锯把墙锯开。

00:18:28.290 --> 00:18:29.930
<v Rose Fuchs>(INTERVIEWER) ELAINE APPLETON
<v Rose Fuchs>（采访者）伊莱恩·阿普尔顿

00:18:29.930 --> 00:18:52.720
<v Rose Fuchs>He was ready to go get a chainsaw and cut the wall down because they knew I wasn't moving, and I wasn't allowing her to get up. I didn't care if it was EMS and their truck. I was not giving over my place of authority, because I did not see that they had adequate knowledge or understanding of the situation.
<v Rose Fuchs>他准备去拿电锯把墙锯开，因为他们知道我不会动，我也不会让她起来。我不在乎那是急救人员还是他们的车。我不会交出我的指挥权，因为我不认为他们对情况有足够的了解或理解。

00:18:52.720 --> 00:19:23.920
<v Rose Fuchs>(HOST) ELAINE APPLETON
<v Rose Fuchs>（主持人）伊莱恩·阿普尔顿

00:19:23.920 --> 00:20:02.720
<v Rose Fuchs>We always count to three, and then we all pull together, like a bunch of he-men. So he's like, who's going to count? And how are we going to do this? Are you going to count? Who's going to count? They just kept on like that. And I looked at my nurse, and I said, you count. So she took over. Like, she heard me say that, and that was it. And I hate to get real loud on this mic, but she proceeded to just roar. We're going to count. 1, 2, 3! And honestly, I think she almost single-handedly pulled me, Katie over to the gurney, with them helping some.
<v Rose Fuchs>我们总是数到三，然后像一群大汉一样一起用力。所以他说，谁来数？我们怎么做？你来数？谁来数？他们就这样一直磨蹭。我看着我的护士，我说，你来数。所以她接手了。她听到我这么说，就是这样。我不想在这个麦克风前大喊大叫，但她开始咆哮。我们要数了。1，2，3！老实说，我认为她几乎是单枪匹马地把我和凯蒂拉到了担架上，他们也帮了一些忙。

00:20:02.720 --> 00:20:41.810
<v Rose Fuchs>(HOST) ELAINE APPLETON
<v Rose Fuchs>（主持人）伊莱恩·阿普尔顿

00:20:41.810 --> 00:21:02.090
<v Rose Fuchs>It's a very bumpy road. These are the back roads of the county, right? So potholes, bumps, very curvy. At times, I thought I was going to fall off the gurney. And the paramedic would grab the waistband on the back of my skirt and hold me so I would hold steady. And I needed to hold steady. My hand had to hold steady.
<v Rose Fuchs>路非常颠簸。这是县里的乡间小路，对吧？所以有坑洼、颠簸，非常弯曲。有时，我觉得我要从担架上掉下来了。医护人员会抓住我裙子后面的腰带稳住我，这样我就能保持平稳。我需要保持平稳。我的手必须保持平稳。

00:21:02.090 --> 00:21:36.170
<v Rose Fuchs>And her leg, at one point, slipped off the gurney and sort of fell down. And so I grabbed her leg and pulled it back up, and I was holding her leg on the gurney with me. So it was a tight squeeze. I was beginning to feel exhausted. I myself don't have the best back, and this was a very awkward position. And your legs are all curled up under you. So you're in an intense kneeling-- sitting on your legs, squatting. So my legs are falling asleep. My hand is starting to fall asleep from the angle that I'm at.
<v Rose Fuchs>有一次她的腿从担架上滑了下来，垂了下去。所以我抓住她的腿拉了回来，我把她的腿和我在担架上一起托住。这地方很挤。我开始感到精疲力竭。我自己的背也不好，这是一个非常尴尬的姿势。你的腿都蜷在身下。所以你处于一种强烈的跪姿——跪在你的腿上，蹲着。所以我的腿开始麻了。我的手因为角度问题也开始麻了。

00:21:36.170 --> 00:21:45.970
<v Rose Fuchs>(HOST) ELAINE APPLETON
<v Rose Fuchs>（主持人）伊莱恩·阿普尔顿

00:21:45.970 --> 00:22:06.370
<v Rose Fuchs>But my hand would periodically feel like it was completely numb. And I would worry, like, am I going to be pinching this cord? And then it would wake up again intermittently. But it was painful, like when your hand falls asleep and it's very painful. It was becoming physically very painful to continue.
<v Rose Fuchs>但我的手会时不时感觉完全麻木。我会担心，我会不会在掐断这根脐带？然后它会间歇性地醒过来。但这很痛苦，就像手麻了之后那种非常痛苦的感觉。继续这样做在身体上变得非常痛苦。

00:22:06.370 --> 00:22:25.110
<v Rose Fuchs>(HOST) ELAINE APPLETON
<v Rose Fuchs>（主持人）伊莱恩·阿普尔顿

00:22:25.110 --> 00:22:27.440
<v Dispatcher>LifeFlight still headed to Houlton Regional?
<v Dispatcher>“生命飞行”救援队还在赶往霍尔顿地区吗？

00:22:27.440 --> 00:22:28.250
<v Jason>Correct.
<v Jason>没错。

00:22:28.250 --> 00:22:34.870
<v Jason>(HOST) ELAINE APPLETON
<v Jason>（主持人）伊莱恩·阿普尔顿

00:22:34.870 --> 00:22:39.650
<v Jason>Yeah. They just started spooling up. I'm looking out my window at them. So yeah. That's our plan.
<v Jason>是啊。他们刚刚开始加速。我从窗户看着他们。所以是的。那是我们的计划。

00:22:39.650 --> 00:22:42.930
<v Dispatcher>They are just now leaving the scene, headed to the hospital.
<v Dispatcher>他们现在正离开现场，赶往医院。

00:22:42.930 --> 00:23:06.130
<v Rose Fuchs>I did not agree with that plan at all, because I knew that I could not take another however many minutes it would take to get into LifeFlight, and them to fuel up, and then to stay like that till a C-section can be done at Eastern Maine Hospital in Bangor. There's just no way.
<v Rose Fuchs>我完全不同意那个计划，因为我知道我无法再忍受飞往“生命飞行”救援队、他们加油、然后在班戈的东缅因医院进行剖腹产所需的时间。根本不可能。

00:23:06.130 --> 00:23:31.730
<v Rose Fuchs>(HOST) ELAINE APPLETON
<v Rose Fuchs>（主持人）伊莱恩·阿普尔顿

00:23:31.730 --> 00:23:40.120
<v Monica Tschirhart>I think they woke me up from sleep. So it was one of those, like, ring, ring, phone call. What's going on? OK. I'll be there in five minutes.
<v Monica Tschirhart>我想他们把我从睡梦中叫醒了。所以那是那种叮铃铃的电话。发生什么事了？好的。我五分钟内赶到。

00:23:40.120 --> 00:23:58.920
<v Monica Tschirhart>(HOST) ELAINE APPLETON
<v Monica Tschirhart>（主持人）伊莱恩·阿普尔顿

00:23:58.920 --> 00:24:13.500
<v Monica Tschirhart>But I'm here. At that point, I'm like, no, we'll have the baby here. And let's go ahead and call in the OR team, and we should deliver her here. We still have that capability. And everyone at that point was like, oh, yeah, we can do that.
<v Monica Tschirhart>但我在这里。那时我想，不，我们就在这里生孩子。让我们继续叫手术室团队，我们应该在这里进行分娩。我们仍然有这种能力。那时每个人都说，哦，是的，我们可以那样做。

00:24:13.500 --> 00:24:18.200
<v Monica Tschirhart>(INTERVIEWER) ELAINE APPLETON
<v Monica Tschirhart>（采访者）伊莱恩·阿普尔顿

00:24:18.200 --> 00:24:26.940
<v Monica Tschirhart>Yeah. They didn't even really think about the fact they still had that capability. But at that transition time, they did.
<v Monica Tschirhart>是的。他们甚至没怎么想过他们还有这种能力。但在那个过渡时间，他们想到了。

00:24:26.940 --> 00:25:15.820
<v Monica Tschirhart>(HOST) ELAINE APPLETON
<v Monica Tschirhart>（主持人）伊莱恩·阿普尔顿

00:25:15.820 --> 00:25:27.860
<v Rose Fuchs>And so we pulled up at the ambulance entrance, and the obstetrician came outside to meet us, which I was so glad for, and started taking the history from me while we were unloading.
<v Rose Fuchs>所以我们停在救护车入口处，产科医生出来迎接我们，我对此感到非常高兴，我们在卸下担架时，她开始从我这里获取病史。

00:25:27.860 --> 00:25:50.310
<v Monica Tschirhart>And I was like, hi, I'm Dr. Tschirhart. I'm an OB/GYN. I'm the one on call today. Recommendation is emergency C-section, if it's OK with you. And we're going to roll you right into the operating room and deliver the baby. Dr. Fuchs also was giving me kind of a sign-out about what was happening with her, what her background was, and that she could still feel the cord pulse.
<v Monica Tschirhart>我说，嗨，我是奇尔哈特医生。我是妇产科医生。今天我是值班医生。建议是紧急剖腹产，如果你同意的话。我们会直接把你推进手术室，把孩子生出来。福克斯医生也给了我一些关于她情况的交代，她的背景是什么，以及她还能感觉到脐带搏动。

00:25:50.310 --> 00:26:37.610
<v Monica Tschirhart>(HOST) ELAINE APPLETON
<v Monica Tschirhart>（主持人）伊莱恩·阿普尔顿

00:26:37.610 --> 00:26:47.610
<v Monica Tschirhart>So we basically put the surgical drapes over the top of Dr. Fuchs, threw the Betadine sterilizing fluid on Katie.
<v Monica Tschirhart>所以我们基本上把手术铺巾盖在福克斯医生的头顶，把聚维酮碘消毒液倒在凯蒂身上。

00:26:47.610 --> 00:27:13.650
<v Rose Fuchs>They opened a bottle of iodine and dumped it over her belly, over my top of my arm, my forearm that was near her. So my cheek is laying on Katie's thigh. I have my head sideways, trying to breathe in a little air pocket in this drape. It's a very awkward position, to say the least, for her and me.
<v Rose Fuchs>他们打开一瓶碘酒，倒在她的肚子上，倒在我靠近她手臂的一侧。所以我的脸贴在凯蒂的大腿上。我侧着头，试图在这个铺巾里呼吸一点空气。至少可以说，对她和我来说，这是一个非常尴尬的姿势。

00:27:13.650 --> 00:27:24.910
<v Monica Tschirhart>We have surgical equipment and tubing and electrocautery and suction kind of sitting on top of Dr. Fuchs, because she's right there between Katie's legs.
<v Monica Tschirhart>我们有手术设备、管道、电灼器和吸引器，基本上都堆在福克斯医生身上，因为她就在凯蒂的双腿之间。

00:27:24.910 --> 00:27:27.950
<v Rose Fuchs>I can feel they are putting their tools on my back.
<v Rose Fuchs>我能感觉到他们把工具放在我的背上。

00:27:27.950 --> 00:27:32.850
<v Rose Fuchs>(HOST) ELAINE APPLETON
<v Rose Fuchs>（主持人）伊莱恩·阿普尔顿

00:27:32.850 --> 00:27:58.670
<v Rose Fuchs>And nurses sort of scurried about, looking for a Doppler. They either couldn't find one or they didn't have one available, but they had no Doppler anyway. And so I told her, it's OK, because I can feel the heartbeat. And so it had been 120s. But then at one point, while they were still prepping, it went down to, like, 90. And so I called that out to her. I said, so we're down to 90. You all better hurry.
<v Rose Fuchs>护士们四处乱跑，寻找多普勒仪。他们要么找不到，要么没有，但他们本来就没有多普勒仪。所以我告诉她，没关系，因为我可以感觉到心跳。心跳一度是120多次。但后来在他们准备时，降到了90。所以我喊了出来。我说，现在降到90了。你们最好快点。

00:27:58.670 --> 00:28:02.070
<v Rose Fuchs>(INTERVIEWER) ELAINE APPLETON
<v Rose Fuchs>（采访者）伊莱恩·阿普尔顿

00:28:02.070 --> 00:28:41.200
<v Rose Fuchs>A normal fetal heart rate is 120 to 160. And so we allow a little bit outside that range, but 90 is definitely getting in trouble. It's often the signal that there's an impending disaster. My fear was the next beat was going to be 60 and the next beat nothing. And then I did my usual, which-- I pushed a little harder on the head, tried a little harder to protect the cord, and I felt the heart rate go back up to the 120s. So then I called out again to reassure them, we're back up to the 120. You're OK. We're doing all right.
<v Rose Fuchs>正常胎心率是120到160。所以我们允许一点点超出这个范围，但90绝对是出问题了。这通常是灾难即将发生的信号。我担心下一次心跳是60，再下一次就没了。然后我做了我平时的动作——我用力压了一下头，试图更努力地保护脐带，我感觉到心率又回到了120多次。所以我又喊出来安慰他们，我们回到了120。你们没事。我们做得很好。

00:28:41.200 --> 00:28:43.320
<v Rose Fuchs>(HOST) ELAINE APPLETON
<v Rose Fuchs>（主持人）伊莱恩·阿普尔顿

00:28:43.320 --> 00:28:46.700
<v Monica Tschirhart>As soon as she went to sleep, I made the incision, got down to her uterus.
<v Monica Tschirhart>她一睡着，我就切开了切口，进入了她的子宫。

00:28:46.700 --> 00:29:08.640
<v Rose Fuchs>I could feel them start to cut the skin and the muscle layers to enter the abdomen. And I thought, oh, dear, my fingers are awfully high up there. What if they cut my fingers? I better pull back a little bit at just the right moment, but not too soon to lose the heartbeat. So I withdrew a little bit. And then I could sort of feel them, that they were in the uterus.
<v Rose Fuchs>我能感觉到他们开始切开皮肤和肌肉层进入腹部。我想，噢天哪，我的手指在那里太高了。如果他们切到我的手怎么办？我最好在恰当的时候稍微退后一点，但又不能太早，以免失去心跳。所以我撤回了一点。然后我能感觉到他们进入了子宫。

00:29:08.640 --> 00:29:19.840
<v Monica Tschirhart>And my hand reached down to pull the baby's head out, and our hands touched. So my hand touched Dr. Fuchs's hand as I was lifting the baby's head out of the incision.
<v Monica Tschirhart>我的手伸下去把婴儿的头拉出来，我们的手碰到了。所以我在把孩子头拉出切口时，我的手碰到了福克斯医生的手。

00:29:19.840 --> 00:30:24.860
<v Monica Tschirhart>(HOST) ELAINE APPLETON
<v Monica Tschirhart>（主持人）伊莱恩·阿普尔顿

00:30:24.860 --> 00:30:41.770
<v Katie Gowell>I could see her off to my left, when she was getting worked on. And then when I was in the recovery room, she was still getting worked on, because then that's when they put her in their incubator type thing. I still didn't get to hold her.
<v Katie Gowell>她在接受处理时，我能在左边看到她。然后在我在恢复室时，她还在接受处理，因为那是他们把她放进保温箱之类的时候。我还是没能抱她。

00:30:41.770 --> 00:30:47.030
<v Katie Gowell>(HOST) ELAINE APPLETON
<v Katie Gowell>（主持人）伊莱恩·阿普尔顿

00:30:47.030 --> 00:31:17.250
<v Katie Gowell>I cried. I cried. I didn't know at that time-- I didn't know how she was. I didn't get to see her. I didn't get to know anything. And then I just saw them wheel her away. It's just a-- I don't know how to describe-- it's a very hard thing, especially seeing her leave. I don't know. It's just-- it was hard, very hard.
<v Katie Gowell>我哭了。我哭了。那时我不知道——我不知道她怎么样。我没能见到她。我什么都不知道。然后我就看着他们把她推走。那只是一个——我不知道该怎么形容——这真是太难了，尤其是看着她离开。我不知道。那只是——这很艰难，非常艰难。

00:31:17.250 --> 00:31:51.930
<v Katie Gowell>(HOST) ELAINE APPLETON
<v Katie Gowell>（主持人）伊莱恩·阿普尔顿

00:31:51.930 --> 00:32:27.210
<v Heather Quint>I wanted to be like-- when they closed it down, I wanted to be like, OK, this is what you guys want? This is what you're going to get. But the nurse in me, the person in me-- that's my patient. Hell, yes, I'm going to be there. I threw on my clothes, and I was there. And I went to Bangor with her. And I sat, and I held her hand, and I talked to her the entire time. So, no. Oh. When I got the call, I wanted to be like, figure it out, but not when my patients are involved. I'm like, yep, I'll be right down.
<v Heather Quint>当他们关闭部门时，我当时想说，好吧，这是你们想要的吗？这就是你们将得到的。但作为一名护士，作为一个普通人——那是我的病人。地狱啊，是的，我会在那里。我穿上衣服，我去了。我和她一起去了班戈。我坐在那里，握着她的手，全程一直和她说话。所以，不。哦。当我接到电话时，我本来想说，你们自己搞定吧，但当涉及到我的病人时，我就不会这样。我说，是的，我马上下来。

00:32:27.210 --> 00:33:37.460
<v Heather Quint>(HOST) ELAINE APPLETON
<v Heather Quint>（主持人）伊莱恩·阿普尔顿

00:33:37.460 --> 00:34:01.580
<v Ira Glass>Elaine Appleton Grant. That story was produced by Lilly Sullivan and edited by Phia Bennin. Elaine originally learned about this story from reporting by Emily Hedegard and Stephanie McFeeters of The Maine Monitor. Coming up, raise your hand if you know what Труд -- дело чести means. That's in a minute, comrades, from Chicago Public Radio, when our program continues.
<v Ira Glass>伊莱恩·阿普尔顿·格兰特。那个故事由莉莉·沙利文制作，菲娅·贝宁编辑。伊莱恩最初是从《缅因州观察报》的艾米丽·赫德加德和斯蒂芬妮·麦克菲特斯的报道中了解到这个故事的。接下来，如果你知道“Труд -- дело чести”意味着什么，请举手。稍后回来，同志们，来自芝加哥公共广播电台，当我们的节目继续时。

00:34:01.580 --> 00:34:23.580
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Today's program, "I Couldn't Help but Notice," we have stories where people see a new future arrive, one that is not quite as pleasant as the past. And they can't help themselves. They take action, in some way or another, in their own little corner of the world. We've arrived at act two of our program. Act Two, Conversations About Important Things.
