00:34:23.580 --> 00:34:57.420
<v Ira Glass>So the documentary that won this year's Academy Award is called Mr. Nobody Against Putin. It was co-directed by a Russian guy and an American, Pasha Talankin and David Borenstein. And it's about the propaganda campaign that was rolled out in Russia after Russia invaded Ukraine in 2022 and the shock of that propaganda push, for one person in particular. If you haven't seen the film, it is really worth seeing. And one of the people on staff here at This American Life, Valerie Kipnis, has watched it four times. She'll tell you why. Here's Val.
<v Ira Glass>今年获得奥斯卡金像奖的纪录片名叫《无名者对抗普京》。它由一位俄罗斯人和一位美国人共同执导，帕夏·塔兰金和大卫·博伦斯坦。它是关于2022年俄罗斯入侵乌克兰后在俄罗斯展开的宣传运动，以及对其中一个人来说，这种宣传冲击带来的震惊。如果你没看过这部电影，它真的很值得一看。而在《美国生活》工作的一位工作人员瓦莱丽·基普尼斯已经看了四遍了。她会告诉你原因。这里是瓦尔。

00:34:57.420 --> 00:35:19.420
<v Valerie Kipnis>Here are some scenes from my family growing up. Someone, let's say my mom, would say this to me. Glory to labor! She'd say it in Russian. Then she'd hand me a broom. Or she'd say, Труд -- дело чести. Labor is a matter of honor. She used to say that on Sunday morning when she woke me up for chores.
<v Valerie Kipnis>这是我小时候家里的一些场景。有人，比如说我妈妈，会对我这么说。劳动光荣！她会用俄语说。然后她会递给我一把扫帚。或者她会说，“Труд -- дело чести”。劳动是一种荣誉。她以前在周日早上叫我起床做家务时常这么说。

00:35:19.420 --> 00:35:50.030
<v Valerie Kipnis>These are all propaganda slogans from her upbringing in the Soviet Union. She and my dad both grew up there. My dad would say things like this too. So did my grandmothers. They both live with us. And these lines always got a laugh from each other. Like, my mom would say, Партия -- наш рулевой!. The party is our helmsman. And my grandmother, Bella, would laugh and say, last time I saw that one was on the wall of an auditorium.
<v Valerie Kipnis>这些都是她在苏联成长过程中的宣传口号。她和我爸爸都是在那里长大的。我爸爸也会说这样的话。我的祖母们也是。她们俩都和我们住在一起。而这些台词总是让彼此发笑。比如，我妈妈会说，“Партия -- наш рулевой!”。党是我们的舵手。而我的祖母贝拉会笑笑说，我上次看到这个是在礼堂的墙上。

00:35:50.030 --> 00:36:15.870
<v Valerie Kipnis>Unlike them, I grew up here, which meant I was always behind, always playing catch-up in a household full of inside jokes, always trying to see into this world they had in their heads. I was obsessed with it. So I'd ask the same questions over and over again. And they would answer my questions patiently, kindly, on long car rides, on the subway to school, on our walks near the BQE.
<v Valerie Kipnis>和她们不同，我是在这里长大的，这意味着我总是落后，总是在一个充满内部笑话的家庭里追赶，总是试图看清她们脑子里的世界。我对此着迷。所以我一遍又一遍地问同样的问题。她们会在漫长的车程里、在去学校的地铁上、在我们靠近BQE的人行道上，耐心地、亲切地回答我的问题。

00:36:15.870 --> 00:36:46.110
<v Valerie Kipnis>But I kept going. I wanted to understand what it actually felt like to live surrounded by banners and slogans. My grandmother would shrug and say что было, то было. It just was what it was. It didn't feel special to us. We didn't know another version of life. And then she'd call me by my family nickname, почемучка, which means, roughly, "kid who drives us crazy by asking why all the time."
<v Valerie Kipnis>但我一直追问。我想了解生活在被横幅和口号包围的环境中到底是什么感觉。我的祖母会耸耸肩说“что было, то было”。过去的就是过去了。这对我们来说并不觉得特别。我们不知道另一种生活。然后她会用我的小名“почемучка”称呼我，这大致意思就是“总是问为什么把我们逼疯的孩子”。

00:36:46.110 --> 00:37:09.190
<v Valerie Kipnis>Being a почемучка and all, it's only natural that I watched this new documentary four times-- three times before it won the Oscar, just saying. And the thing I couldn't get enough of was that here was a movie that answered some of the questions I'd been asking for so long. The documentary starts less than a month after Russia invaded Ukraine, the beginning of the full-scale invasion.
<v Valerie Kipnis>作为一个“为什么宝宝”，我看了四遍这部新纪录片是很自然的——在它获得奥斯卡之前看了三遍，我只是提一下。我怎么看都不够的原因是，这部电影回答了我长期以来一直问的一些问题。这部纪录片开始于俄罗斯入侵乌克兰不到一个月后，即全面入侵的开始。

00:37:09.190 --> 00:37:44.550
<v Valerie Kipnis>And it focuses on one school in one town in Russia as the Ministry of Education rolls out a massive, nationwide propaganda campaign, a new, quote, "patriotic curriculum," which would include new lesson plans for teachers, nationalistic poem recitations for students, mandatory school-wide salutes to the flag, and mandatory lessons called Разговоры о важном. Conversations about Important Things, where teachers are asked to talk about stuff like the importance of war songs or the heroism of Russian fighters.
<v Valerie Kipnis>它聚焦于俄罗斯一个城镇的一所学校，当时教育部推出了大规模的、全国性的宣传运动，一项新的、引用，“爱国课程”，其中包括教师的新教案、学生朗诵民族主义诗歌、强制全校向旗帜致敬，以及名为“关于重要事情的谈话”的强制课程。关于重要事情的谈话，教师被要求谈论战争歌曲的重要性或俄罗斯战士的英雄主义。

00:37:44.550 --> 00:38:04.030
<v Valerie Kipnis>Also, a single approved set of history textbooks, written by two people closely affiliated with the Kremlin. High-schoolers would start paramilitary training in gym class. And in the school that's in the documentary, one teacher captures how this all plays out, Pasha Talankin. And I called him up.
<v Valerie Kipnis>此外，还有一套经过批准的历史教科书，由两名与克里姆林宫关系密切的人编写。高中生将在体育课上开始准军事训练。而在纪录片里的那所学校，一位老师帕夏·塔兰金捕捉到了这一切是如何发生的。我打电话给了他。

00:38:04.030 --> 00:38:28.440
<v Valerie Kipnis>Here was a person who made a whole film about what it felt like to see slogans in the auditorium, different slogans than the ones my parents and grandparents grew up with-- a very different time-- but with an undeniable similarity. Both then and now, the state had a tight grip on its own people. And here was Pasha, who wasn't just seeing what was happening around him. He wanted the whole world to see what he was seeing.
<v Valerie Kipnis>他是一个拍了一整部关于在礼堂看到标语是什么感觉的电影的人，标语和我父母祖辈长大的标语不同——这是一个非常不同的时代——但有着不可否认的相似之处。无论过去还是现在，国家对人民都有严格的控制。而在这里的帕夏，他不仅看到了周围发生的事情。他希望全世界看到他所看到的。

00:38:28.440 --> 00:38:33.750
<v Pasha Talankin>[SPEAKING RUSSIAN]
<v Pasha Talankin>[说俄语]

00:38:33.750 --> 00:39:03.840
<v Valerie Kipnis>This is Pasha. He's got a round, wide-eyed baby face, big glasses. He's an impish kind of person who talks through a smile, like he's in on some joke you don't know about. The school in the film is actually the same school he once attended. And he loves the school, loves his students, loves his grumpy mom. She's the school librarian. And when the film begins, he's the school's event coordinator and videographer. He films the piano recitals, graduations, performances.
<v Valerie Kipnis>这是帕夏。他有一张圆圆的、睁大眼睛的婴儿脸，戴着大眼镜。他是一个顽皮的人，说话时带着微笑，就像他知道什么你不知道的笑话一样。电影里的学校其实就是他曾经就读的那所学校。他热爱学校，热爱学生，热爱他那脾气暴躁的妈妈。她是学校图书管理员。当电影开始时，他是学校的活动协调员和摄像师。他拍摄钢琴独奏会、毕业典礼、表演。

00:39:03.840 --> 00:39:32.060
<v Valerie Kipnis>And under the brand-new patriotic curriculum at Pasha's school, Pasha was told he now had a brand-new role. He would be filming teachers and students performing the new lessons. He'd be filming the new flag salutes and paramilitary trainings. And he would be sending all of that footage to the government as proof of compliance. I wanted to know what he thought of his new role as he was doing it, how it actually felt. He answered by telling me a story that never made it into the film.
<v Valerie Kipnis>而在帕夏学校全新的爱国课程下，帕夏被告知他现在有了全新的角色。他将拍摄教师和学生进行新课程的表演。他将拍摄新的旗帜致敬仪式和准军事训练。他将把所有这些片段发送给政府作为合规证明。我想知道他在做这些时是怎么想的，实际感觉如何。他通过给我讲一个从未进入电影的故事回答了我。

00:39:32.060 --> 00:39:37.920
<v Pasha Talankin>[SPEAKING RUSSIAN]
<v Pasha Talankin>[说俄语]

00:39:37.920 --> 00:39:53.280
<v Interpreter>What was I thinking about when I filmed this? I mean, at first, I was just-- how do you say this in Russia? I started sweating under my visor. I just lost my mind about what was happening. I just totally freaked out.
<v Interpreter>我拍这个的时候在想什么？我的意思是，起初，我只是——你在俄罗斯怎么说这个？我开始在面罩下出汗。我简直疯了，不知道发生了什么。我完全吓坏了。

00:39:53.280 --> 00:39:55.450
<v Pasha Talankin>[SPEAKING RUSSIAN]
<v Pasha Talankin>[说俄语]

00:39:55.450 --> 00:40:02.760
<v Interpreter>When I was going to film computer class-- it didn't end up in the final cut-- I asked myself a question.
<v Interpreter>当我准备去拍计算机课时——它没有出现在最终剪辑中——我问了自己一个问题。

00:40:02.760 --> 00:40:04.740
<v Pasha Talankin>[SPEAKING RUSSIAN]
<v Pasha Talankin>[说俄语]

00:40:04.740 --> 00:40:39.930
<v Interpreter>Who is that idiot who was going to be watching all this shit? Who is he? Who is that moron? I mean, it should be some sort of paid job, sitting and watching all this shit. They should pay you some money for that, at least a little bit. Anyway, I did this one little bit of trickery, where I left in only the beginning of the class, where the teacher says, hello, kids, and the end, where they say, OK, class is over. But the shot itself went the whole time of the class, 45 minutes. So in the middle, there was just a black screen, as if something had gone wrong with my camera.
<v Interpreter>那个要看这些烂东西的白痴是谁？他是谁？那个笨蛋是谁？我的意思是，这应该是一种付费工作，坐着看这些垃圾。他们至少应该为此付点钱。总之，我耍了个小聪明，只保留了课堂的开头，老师说，孩子们好，以及结尾，他们说，好的，下课了。但拍摄本身持续了整节课，45分钟。所以中间只有黑屏，好像我的摄像机出了问题一样。

00:40:39.930 --> 00:40:41.610
<v Pasha Talankin>[SPEAKING RUSSIAN]
<v Pasha Talankin>[说俄语]

00:40:41.610 --> 00:40:57.650
<v Interpreter>And I sent the video in. And I sat there waiting as they looked over the footage. And I kept waiting for them to say, we can't see it. Something went wrong. It's just a black screen. And they didn't give me a single note. And I thought--
<v Interpreter>然后我把视频发了过去。我坐在那里等着，他们检查了片段。我一直等着他们说，我们看不到。出了问题。只是个黑屏。他们没给我任何反馈。我想——

00:40:57.650 --> 00:41:00.930
<v Pasha Talankin>[SPEAKING RUSSIAN]
<v Pasha Talankin>[说俄语]

00:41:00.930 --> 00:41:20.510
<v Interpreter>--holy shit. I'm here not to actually record, but to surveil the teachers. Watch out. We're watching you. We have a camera. Here it is, recording you. And in that moment, I thought, I'm being used.
<v Interpreter>——天哪。我在这里不是为了记录，而是为了监视老师。小心。我们在看着你。我们有摄像机。在这里，记录着你们。在那一刻，我想，我正在被利用。

00:41:20.510 --> 00:41:22.370
<v Pasha Talankin>[SPEAKING RUSSIAN]
<v Pasha Talankin>[说俄语]

00:41:22.370 --> 00:41:25.050
<v Interpreter>I'm being used.
<v Interpreter>我正在被利用。

00:41:25.050 --> 00:41:43.450
<v Valerie Kipnis>He figured, yeah, OK, they were using him. But he was simultaneously and obsessively filming the changes at his school for his own purposes. And the footage that Pasha captured was really eye-opening for me. It answered so many of the questions I'd badgered my family with over the years.
<v Valerie Kipnis>他想，是的，好吧，他们正在利用他。但他同时又强迫症般地为了自己的目的拍摄学校的变化。而帕夏捕捉到的镜头真的让我大开眼界。它回答了我在过去几年里一直缠着家人问的许多问题。

00:41:43.450 --> 00:42:13.090
<v Valerie Kipnis>Because the propaganda, as it rolls out, is sort of the opposite of what I'd always imagined it would be, you know, the classic images of state control-- the seamless, crisp obedience, massive signs, arms being raised in unison, echoey speeches delivered to neat rows of thousands of people. Pasha shows scenes that are so intimate and small and messy, a teacher stumbling over new words she's now required to say, denazification, demilitarization.
<v Valerie Kipnis>因为宣传，随着它的展开，和我一直想象的某种相反，你知道，国家控制的经典形象——无缝、干脆的服从，巨大的标语，手臂整齐地抬起，在成千上万人的整齐队列中发表回响的演讲。帕夏展示的场景是如此私密、微小且混乱，一位老师在结结巴巴地念她现在被要求说的那些新词，去纳粹化，去军事化。

00:42:13.090 --> 00:42:17.130
<v Teacher>[SPEAKING RUSSIAN]
<v Teacher>[说俄语]

00:42:17.130 --> 00:42:22.830
<v Valerie Kipnis>He films teenagers, bored out of their minds, staring blindly into space as another teacher tells them--
<v Valerie Kipnis>他拍下青少年们，无聊透顶，茫然地盯着空间，另一位老师告诉他们——

00:42:22.830 --> 00:42:27.730
<v Teacher>[SPEAKING RUSSIAN]
<v Teacher>[说俄语]

00:42:27.730 --> 00:42:40.750
<v Valerie Kipnis>If you were born in this country and you don't believe we're doing the right thing, then leave. Go to the country that you think is better. Pasha films young kids with these tiny red berets, chanting new slogans in an assembly.
<v Valerie Kipnis>如果你出生在这个国家，你不相信我们正在做正确的事，那就离开。去你认为更好的国家。帕夏拍下了戴着这些红色小贝雷帽的年轻孩子，在集会上高呼新口号。

00:42:40.750 --> 00:42:45.930
<v Children>[SPEAKING RUSSIAN]
<v Children>[说俄语]

00:42:45.930 --> 00:43:17.860
<v Valerie Kipnis>They're excited, but looking at each other, like, right? We're saying the right thing? The school days in the film look kind of normal in so many ways, despite all the changes. Kids still horse around and tease each other. There are still piano recitals. It's still just school. But Pasha was like the kid in the Emperor's New Clothes. Every change stood out to him. And he couldn't stop talking about it. His own mother was like, of course these things are happening. They've always been this way. Shut up. And he argued with her.
<v Valerie Kipnis>他们很兴奋，但看着对方，就像，对吗？我们说的是对的吗？电影里的上学日子在许多方面看起来很正常，尽管有所有的变化。孩子们仍然打闹、互相取笑。仍然有钢琴独奏会。这仍然只是上学。但帕夏就像《皇帝的新衣》里的那个孩子。每一个变化在他眼中都显得突出。他无法停止谈论它。他自己的妈妈说，这些事情当然会发生。它们一直都是这样。闭嘴。而他和她争论。

00:43:17.860 --> 00:43:43.060
<v Valerie Kipnis>He lived in a sort of continuous state of shock about the war, about the propaganda. And he told me that when the patriotic curriculum started, he kept sort of waiting for the people around him to freak out as much as he was about what they were being asked to do. But most people just went along with it, adapted, or chose to stay quiet. I wondered why he didn't do that. Then, when I talked to him, I got it.
<v Valerie Kipnis>他处于一种持续的战争冲击状态，关于宣传。他告诉我，当爱国课程开始时，他一直等着周围的人像他一样对他们被要求做的事情感到震惊。但大多数人只是随波逐流，适应，或选择保持沉默。我好奇为什么他不那样做。然后，当我与他交谈时，我明白了。

00:43:43.060 --> 00:44:12.100
<v Valerie Kipnis>For starters, Pasha and I are almost the same age, in our 30s. And part of why he's so stunned by what's happening is because of the era he grew up in. It was right after the Soviet Union fell apart, but before Vladimir Putin really started consolidating power. So Pasha was raised in this short window of time when students weren't flooded with slogans and propaganda in the schools, when schools were left mostly untouched by the state apparatus, because the state apparatus had fallen apart.
<v Valerie Kipnis>首先，帕夏和我年纪差不多，都在30多岁。他之所以对正在发生的事情感到如此震惊，部分原因是他成长的时代。那是在苏联解体之后，但在普京真正开始巩固权力之前。所以帕夏是在一个短暂的时间窗口里长大的，那时学生们没有被学校里的口号和宣传所淹没，那时学校大部分没有受到国家机器的干扰，因为国家机器已经解体了。

00:44:12.100 --> 00:44:30.260
<v Valerie Kipnis>And so what Pasha was seeing was something my parents and grandparents never got to see-- the beginning of a propaganda push in the schools, which meant he understood what his school was losing when it adopted these changes. And he knew what he was personally losing as nationalism became more and more strictly enforced.
<v Valerie Kipnis>所以帕夏所看到的，是我父母和祖辈从未见过的——学校里宣传攻势的开始，这意味着他明白当他的学校采用这些变化时正在失去什么。他知道当民族主义变得越来越严格执行时，他个人失去了什么。

00:44:30.260 --> 00:44:31.860
<v Pasha Talankin>[SPEAKING RUSSIAN]
<v Pasha Talankin>[说俄语]

00:44:31.860 --> 00:44:41.420
<v Valerie Kipnis>Over and over again in the film, he addresses people in his life in the camera directly. Like, can you believe this? Why is this happening? Potchemy.
<v Valerie Kipnis>在电影中，他一次又一次地直接对着摄像机对着他生活中的人说话。比如，你相信吗？为什么会发生这种情况？为什么？

00:44:41.420 --> 00:44:46.720
<v Valerie Kipnis>He told me that off camera, he had many more exchanges like that, especially with the other teachers.
<v Valerie Kipnis>他告诉我，在镜头外，他有更多那样的交流，特别是和其他老师。

00:44:46.720 --> 00:44:48.300
<v Pasha Talankin>[SPEAKING RUSSIAN]
<v Pasha Talankin>[说俄语]

00:44:48.300 --> 00:45:01.280
<v Interpreter>The teachers came to me. They said, Pasha, we don't like all this. And then they come into class and started to say what the Ministry of Education had sent over to them.
<v Interpreter>老师们来找我。他们说，帕夏，我们不喜欢这一切。然后他们走进教室，开始说教育部发给他们的那些话。

00:45:01.280 --> 00:45:02.820
<v Pasha Talankin>[SPEAKING RUSSIAN]
<v Pasha Talankin>[说俄语]

00:45:02.820 --> 00:45:08.580
<v Interpreter>The teachers are hostages of this system too.
<v Interpreter>老师们也是这个系统的受害者。

00:45:08.580 --> 00:45:25.510
<v Valerie Kipnis>Pasha also saw himself as a hostage. Before the war and the new curriculum, Pasha was the teacher with the Pussy Riot poster on his wall, the cool guy, the weirdo whose office all the theater kids hung out in, the teacher who had different politics than what was on TV every night.
<v Valerie Kipnis>帕夏也把自己视为受害者。在战争和新课程之前，帕夏是墙上挂着Pussy Riot海报的老师，酷哥，那个所有戏剧孩子都在他办公室里闲逛的怪胎，那个政见与每晚电视上播放的内容不同的老师。

00:45:25.510 --> 00:45:50.270
<v Valerie Kipnis>And for a while, even after the war was underway, he was able to continue being that guy. He wore blue and yellow paperclips on his clothing as symbols of support for Ukraine. He hung an image of an anti-war flag in his office, flipped a Russian flag outside the school upside down. One time, he even played Lady Gaga singing "The Star-Spangled Banner" during a flag assembly instead of the Russian national anthem.
<v Valerie Kipnis>有一段时间，即使在战争正在进行时，他仍然能够保持那个样子。他在衣服上戴着蓝黄色回形针作为支持乌克兰的象征。他在办公室挂了一张反战旗帜的图片，把学校外面的俄罗斯国旗倒挂。有一次，他甚至在国旗集会上播放了Lady Gaga唱的《星条旗永不落》，而不是俄罗斯国歌。

00:45:50.270 --> 00:46:04.870
<v Valerie Kipnis>In the movie, he does things that I could not believe he was getting away with. In a country that was introducing laws to criminalize things, like even saying that the war was, in fact, a war, how on Earth could he just do these things and not get in trouble?
<v Valerie Kipnis>在电影里，他做的事情让我无法相信他居然能逃脱惩罚。在一个引入法律将诸如承认战争实际上是一场战争之类的行为定为犯罪的国家，他怎么可能就这样做而不惹上麻烦？

00:46:04.870 --> 00:46:07.470
<v Pasha Talankin>[SPEAKING RUSSIAN]
<v Pasha Talankin>[说俄语]

00:46:07.470 --> 00:46:09.690
<v Interpreter>People sort of saw me as--
<v Interpreter>人们有点看我像——

00:46:09.690 --> 00:46:11.350
<v Pasha Talankin>[SPEAKING RUSSIAN]
<v Pasha Talankin>[说俄语]

00:46:11.350 --> 00:46:19.190
<v Interpreter>--a jester, a holy fool. There he goes, walking around, being foolish.
<v Interpreter>——一个小丑，一个神圣的傻瓜。你看那儿，到处走动，耍宝。

00:46:19.190 --> 00:46:22.630
<v Pasha Talankin>[SPEAKING RUSSIAN]
<v Pasha Talankin>[说俄语]

00:46:22.630 --> 00:46:37.090
<v Interpreter>In Russia, there is this historical tradition that the jester, the holy fool, could speak out, even to the tzar. The lowest level of society, the most seemingly worthless people, they could say exactly what they thought, tell anyone right to their face.
<v Interpreter>在俄罗斯，有这样一种历史传统，小丑、神圣的傻瓜，即使是对沙皇也可以直言不讳。社会最底层，看起来最无用的人，他们可以确切地说出自己的想法，对着任何人的脸说出来。

00:46:37.090 --> 00:46:39.430
<v Pasha Talankin>[SPEAKING RUSSIAN]
<v Pasha Talankin>[说俄语]

00:46:39.430 --> 00:47:08.130
<v Valerie Kipnis>But that didn't mean the tzars would just ignore him forever. When I talked to Pasha about how his constant provoking was playing out in real life, he told me that behind the scenes of making the film, he could feel his status changing from holy fool to persona non grata. This isn't in the film, but at some point, at Pasha's school, compliance started being supervised in person via a new, quote, "advisor on student affairs." And this advisor took a special interest in Pasha.
<v Valerie Kipnis>但这并不意味着沙皇会永远忽视他。当我与帕夏谈论他不断挑衅在现实生活中是如何上演时，他告诉我，在制作电影的幕后，他能感觉到自己的地位从神圣的傻瓜变成了不受欢迎的人。电影里没提，但在帕夏学校的某个时刻，合规开始通过一名新的、引用，“学生事务顾问”进行现场监督。而这位顾问对帕夏特别感兴趣。

00:47:08.130 --> 00:47:11.890
<v Pasha Talankin>[SPEAKING RUSSIAN]
<v Pasha Talankin>[说俄语]

00:47:11.890 --> 00:47:44.240
<v Interpreter>This advisor was supposed to monitor student activity on social media, establish contact with them, basically sniff everything out. And so one day, this advisor came to me and said, I'm going to close your little bullshit factory. Your office in other words, right? I'm going to close it. And so I took my door off its hinges and put it in front of her door and said, how are you going to close this bullshit factory if it doesn't have any doors?
<v Interpreter>这位顾问本应监视社交媒体上的学生动态，与他们建立联系，基本上嗅出一切。所以有一天，这位顾问来找我说，我要关闭你的小混蛋工厂。换句话说，就是你的办公室，对吧？我要关闭它。所以我把门从铰链上取下来放在她的门前，说，如果这个混蛋工厂没有门，你要怎么关闭它？

00:47:44.240 --> 00:47:47.260
<v Pasha Talankin>[SPEAKING RUSSIAN]
<v Pasha Talankin>[说俄语]

00:47:47.260 --> 00:48:01.880
<v Interpreter>But, of course, this performance eventually ended, and I returned the door to its place. But in the meantime, she got really committed to this work of telling the students not to come see me.
<v Interpreter>当然，这场戏最终结束了，我把门放回了原处。但与此同时，她真的致力于这项工作，告诉学生们不要来找我。

00:48:01.880 --> 00:48:22.040
<v Valerie Kipnis>And fewer kids were showing up at his office. The domain he'd carved out inside the school-- not just the physical space of his office, but the permission he represented for kids who wanted to joke around, talk freely, ask questions, and for him to be able to do that-- that was being shrunk down to nothing.
<v Valerie Kipnis>而出现在他办公室的孩子越来越少。他在学校里开辟的领域——不仅仅是办公室的物理空间，还有他为那些想开玩笑、自由交谈、提问题的孩子所代表的许可，以及他能够那样做的能力——正在缩减到零。

00:48:22.040 --> 00:48:54.440
<v Valerie Kipnis>When I asked Pasha what this period of his life felt like, he gave me an answer that I can't help but classify as literally the most Russian thing I've ever heard. He said it was like this one painting he loves, Barge Haulers on the Volga, which is an oil-on-canvas painting by Ilya Repin. It depicts 11 men hauling a barge along the banks of a river. These men are at the point of collapse from exhaustion, oppressed by heavy, hot weather, leaning into these big leather straps attached to the boat they're pulling. They're exhausted, covered in dirt.
<v Valerie Kipnis>当我问帕夏他人生的这段时期是什么感觉时，他给我的回答我情不自禁地认为是我听过的字面上最俄罗斯的东西。他说这就像他喜爱的一幅画，《伏尔加河上的纤夫》，那是伊利亚·列宾的油画。它描绘了11个人沿着河岸拉着一艘驳船。这些人因为精疲力竭、被沉重炎热的天气压迫，倾身在连接着他们拉的船的巨大的皮带上，处于崩溃的边缘。他们筋疲力尽，浑身是泥。

00:48:54.440 --> 00:49:08.380
<v Valerie Kipnis>But among them stands a younger man, whose face is in the path of the sun. He looks in a different direction. He looks up. And his body refuses to pull the boat. He seems to be the only one questioning the work they're being asked to do.
<v Valerie Kipnis>但在他们之中站着一个年轻人，他的脸正对着阳光。他看向不同的方向。他往上看。他的身体拒绝拉船。他似乎是唯一一个质疑他们被要求做的这项工作的人。

00:49:08.380 --> 00:49:09.600
<v Pasha Talankin>[SPEAKING RUSSIAN]
<v Pasha Talankin>[说俄语]

00:49:09.600 --> 00:49:18.000
<v Interpreter>Like, why are we doing all this? And the rest of them just keep their head down, keep pulling this ship along the Volga--
<v Interpreter>就像，我们为什么要做这一切？而其余的人只是低下头，继续沿着伏尔加河拉这艘船——

00:49:18.000 --> 00:49:19.840
<v Pasha Talankin>[SPEAKING RUSSIAN]
<v Pasha Talankin>[说俄语]

00:49:19.840 --> 00:49:21.340
<v Interpreter>--as if they don't hear what he's saying.
<v Interpreter>——好像他们没听到他在说什么。

00:49:21.340 --> 00:49:24.720
<v Pasha Talankin>[SPEAKING RUSSIAN]
<v Pasha Talankin>[说俄语]

00:49:24.720 --> 00:50:00.640
<v Interpreter>It's like you're speaking different languages and you won't understand each other. You don't have points of contact. You don't have common views on basically any questions. You can't discuss issues that demand discussion. They require it, not because you can change something, but because you need to not be alone. You need to speak in order to feel support, to find at least one person that you can talk about this with. But there's no one.
<v Interpreter>这就像你在说不同的语言，你们不会理解对方。你们没有共同点。你们在基本任何问题上都没有共同观点。你们无法讨论需要讨论的问题。他们需要它，不是因为你可以改变什么，而是因为你需要不孤单。你需要说话才能感觉到支持，找到至少一个你可以谈论这件事的人。但没有一个人。

00:50:00.640 --> 00:50:26.710
<v Valerie Kipnis>There is one last scene in the film that really got to me. It's so inside in a way that lets you really feel how much has changed in this one school, this one town. Pasha gets invited to this party of a recent graduate, Vanya, who's heading off to the army. It's his going away party. All his friends toast to him and wish him luck in the future. They pose for pictures with Vanya. Instead of "cheese," they say, "army!"
<v Valerie Kipnis>电影中有一个最后的场景真的打动了我。它是如此的内在，以至于让你真正感受到这所学校、这个城镇发生了多少变化。帕夏受邀参加了一位应届毕业生瓦尼亚的派对，他正要去参军。这是他的告别派对。他所有的朋友为他干杯，祝他未来好运。他们和瓦尼亚拍照。他们不说“茄子”，而是说“军队！”

00:50:26.710 --> 00:50:27.810
<v Partygoers>[SPEAKING RUSSIAN]
<v Partygoers>[说俄语]

00:50:27.810 --> 00:50:32.510
<v Valerie Kipnis>Then they take turns shaving his head with a shaver that breaks halfway through the process.
<v Valerie Kipnis>然后他们轮流用一把在过程中坏掉的剃须刀剃他的头。

00:50:32.510 --> 00:50:34.900
<v Man>[SPEAKING RUSSIAN]
<v Man>[说俄语]

00:50:34.900 --> 00:50:38.210
<v Man>[SHAVER BUZZING]
<v Man>[剃须刀嗡嗡声]

00:50:38.210 --> 00:50:44.790
<v Valerie Kipnis>Pasha takes a turn at shaving Vanya's head. As he does it, he asks, you decided to sign up on your own?
<v Valerie Kipnis>帕夏轮到给瓦尼亚剃头。当他做的时候，他问，你决定自己报名参军？

00:50:44.790 --> 00:50:46.450
<v Pasha Talankin>[SPEAKING RUSSIAN]
<v Pasha Talankin>[说俄语]

00:50:46.450 --> 00:51:12.690
<v Valerie Kipnis>Vanya says, didn't have time. They sent for me. Pasha says, why didn't you call me? Vanya sort of shrugs, as if Pasha's question is dumb and annoying. But then, quietly, he mumbles, it just all happened so fast, in a split second. Besides that, it's all laughs and jokes and debauchery. The shaving part is funny. Everyone's kind of wasted.
<v Valerie Kipnis>瓦尼亚说，没时间了。他们送我去了。帕夏说，为什么不打电话给我？瓦尼亚耸了耸肩，好像帕夏的问题很蠢很烦人。但后来，他轻轻地嘟囔说，这一切发生得太快了，就在一瞬间。除此之外，全是欢笑、笑话和颓废。剃头部分很有趣。大家都喝得有点多了。

00:51:12.690 --> 00:51:28.310
<v Valerie Kipnis>But then as it gets late, Vanya and his friends start embracing. One guy cries and hugs him, tells him that he really loves him so much, that he won't forget him. He tells him, you're like a brother to me, not by blood, but by soul.
<v Valerie Kipnis>但后来天晚了，瓦尼亚和他的朋友们开始拥抱。一个人哭了，拥抱他，告诉他他真的很爱他，他不会忘记他。他告诉他，你对我来说就像兄弟，不是血缘，而是灵魂。

00:51:28.310 --> 00:51:30.570
<v Man>[SPEAKING RUSSIAN]
<v Man>[说俄语]

00:51:30.570 --> 00:52:16.700
<v Valerie Kipnis>Vanya doesn't look so brave in the dark. He looks small and scared. His friends also do. And that's when it hit me. Before now, Vanya and the others seem like they maybe believed in this war, like they wouldn't mind going to fight for Russia. But in this moment, it feels like you can see everyone grasp the realness of the thing they'd been saying, and then immediately understand that there was now no turning back, like a joke gone too far. Because at this point, so many Russians have gone to fight this war, and well over 100,000 Ukrainians have been killed in it.
<v Valerie Kipnis>瓦尼亚在黑暗中看起来没那么勇敢。他看起来又小又怕。他的朋友们也一样。就在那时，我明白了。在此之前，瓦尼亚和其他人似乎可能相信这场战争，好像他们不介意去为俄罗斯战斗。但在那一刻，感觉你可以看到每个人都抓住了他们一直在说的事情的真实性，然后立即明白现在没有回头路了，就像一个开过火的玩笑。因为此时，这么多俄罗斯人已经去打这场战争了，而超过10万乌克兰人在其中丧生。

00:52:16.700 --> 00:52:49.820
<v Valerie Kipnis>Do people believe the propaganda? I wondered this question over and over again as I was watching. Does Vanya actually believe in the war he's going to fight? Do the teachers believe that Russia is denazifying Ukraine? Do the kids throwing fake grenades believe in the country they are being trained to defend? It's a version of the question I've been asking my family my whole life. But did you believe in the things you were being taught, the things you were doing, the slogans hung up in the gym? Those are the questions they seem the most frustrated by.
<v Valerie Kipnis>人们相信宣传吗？我在观看时一遍又一遍地问这个问题。瓦尼亚真的相信他要去打的这场战争吗？老师们相信俄罗斯正在对乌克兰进行去纳粹化吗？那些投掷假手榴弹的孩子们相信他们被训练去保卫的国家吗？这是我一生都在问我家人的那个问题的变体。但你们相信你们被教导的事情，你们正在做的事情，挂在健身房里的标语吗？那些似乎是让他们感到最沮丧的问题。

00:52:49.820 --> 00:53:13.120
<v Valerie Kipnis>It took me asking Pasha to figure out why. When I asked him if the people around him believe in all this, he scoffed at me, the way my grandma does, like, that's a bad question, you dummy journalist. Don't you understand? He told me, it doesn't matter if people believe in it or not. What matters is that they say it, that they hang the slogans, teach the lessons, sing the anthems.
<v Valerie Kipnis>我问帕夏才弄明白原因。当我问他周围的人是否相信这一切时，他像我祖母那样嘲笑我，就像，这是一个坏问题，你这个笨蛋记者。你不明白吗？他告诉我，人们信不信并不重要。重要的是他们说了，他们挂了标语，教了课程，唱了国歌。

00:53:13.120 --> 00:53:15.020
<v Pasha Talankin>[SPEAKING RUSSIAN]
<v Pasha Talankin>[说俄语]

00:53:15.020 --> 00:53:21.560
<v Interpreter>First, it's a routine that's like, I believe, I don't believe, but I say it.
<v Interpreter>首先，这是一种常规，就像，我信，我不信，但我说了。

00:53:21.560 --> 00:53:23.580
<v Pasha Talankin>[SPEAKING RUSSIAN]
<v Pasha Talankin>[说俄语]

00:53:23.580 --> 00:53:34.320
<v Interpreter>Then it becomes culture. These lessons are already in the schedule. Everyone's used to it, like, you got to say this. You got to listen to that. You got to raise the flag or whatever else.
<v Interpreter>然后它变成了文化。这些课程已经在日程表上了。每个人都习惯了，比如，你得说这个。你得听那个。你得升旗或做其他事。

00:53:34.320 --> 00:53:36.100
<v Pasha Talankin>[SPEAKING RUSSIAN]
<v Pasha Talankin>[说俄语]

00:53:36.100 --> 00:53:44.660
<v Interpreter>And then it becomes a cage, and you think this is normal. And I'd also add that the cage actually remains open.
<v Interpreter>然后它变成了一个笼子，你认为这是正常的。我还要补充一点，笼子实际上保持打开状态。

00:53:44.660 --> 00:53:46.340
<v Pasha Talankin>[SPEAKING RUSSIAN]
<v Pasha Talankin>[说俄语]

00:53:46.340 --> 00:54:10.860
<v Interpreter>And you can leave it at any moment. But as soon as you do, you realize that everyone else is in the cage. You walk out, and you realize that you've become an outcast, a loner, that your opinion, you, yourself, you don't matter to anyone. And that's also the work of propaganda. That's what it does.
<v Interpreter>你可以随时离开它。但一旦你离开，你就会意识到其他人都在笼子里。你走出去，你意识到你已经成了局外人，一个孤独的人，你的意见，你自己，你对任何人都不重要。这也就是宣传的工作。这就是它所做的。

00:54:10.860 --> 00:54:30.900
<v Valerie Kipnis>Pasha became more and more isolated, retreated from his friends and family. They stayed away from him too. An unmarked vehicle showed up outside his home. And he got scared and left the country without telling anyone where he was going or why. And he can't go back.
<v Valerie Kipnis>帕夏变得越来越孤立，退出了他的朋友和家人。他们也远离了他。一辆没有标记的车辆出现在他家门外。他害怕了，没告诉任何人他要去哪或为什么就离开了这个国家。他回不去了。

00:54:30.900 --> 00:54:52.260
<v Valerie Kipnis>I should probably fess up now and say, all those times I was asking my family about how they felt, growing up, when I was posing those exact questions to Pasha, in the back of my mind, I was always wondering, who would I be if I was put in that situation, what kind of person? Would I be able to see what was happening and push back?
<v Valerie Kipnis>我现在可能应该坦白说，所有那些我问家人他们成长感受的时候，当我对帕夏提出那些确切的问题时，在我的脑海里，我总是想，如果我处在那样的境地，我会是谁，什么样的人？我能看到发生了什么并反击吗？

00:54:52.260 --> 00:55:13.910
<v Valerie Kipnis>When I saw the film and when I talked to Pasha, I found all these similarities between us. We're both similar ages, similar tendency to ask questions. And I did think-- quietly, to myself-- that had I been put in the situation, I probably would be like Pasha. I told my dad this-- big mistake.
<v Valerie Kipnis>当我看到这部电影并与帕夏交谈时，我发现了我们之间的所有相似之处。我们年纪相仿，有类似的提问倾向。我确实想——悄悄对自己说——如果我处在那种境地，我可能会像帕夏一样。我告诉了我爸爸——大错特错。

00:55:13.910 --> 00:55:48.410
<v Valerie Kipnis>He said, really? You'd be like that? What are you doing right now in this country? There are things happening here that you don't like. Then I said, I'm doing my best, I think. Then he said, well, isn't that great? Your best. What's that got to do with anything?
<v Valerie Kipnis>他说，真的吗？你会像那样？你现在在这个国家做什么？这里发生了一些你不喜欢的事情。然后我说，我在尽我所能，我想。然后他说，那不是太棒了吗？你的尽力。那跟任何事情有什么关系？

00:55:48.410 --> 00:56:04.350
<v Ira Glass>Valerie Kipnis is one of the producers of our show. The actor who's reading Pasha's quotes in English was E. Lia Kleinbock.
<v Ira Glass>瓦莱丽·基普尼斯是我们节目的制作人之一。阅读帕夏英文引语的演员是E.利亚·克莱因博克。

00:56:04.350 --> 00:56:27.710
<v Ira Glass>Well, our program was produced today by Nadia Reiman and edited by Nancy Updike. The people who put together today's show include Chana Joffe-Walt, Adriene Lilly, Tobin Low, Molly Marcello, Katherine Rae Mondo, Stowe Nelson, Ruthie Petitto, Ryan Rumery, Ruby Schwartz, Frances Swanson, Christopher Swetala, Marisa Robertson-Textor, Julie Whitaker, and Diane Wu. Our managing editor is Sarah Abdurrahman, Senior Editor, David Kestenbaum. Our Executive Editor is Emanuele Berry.
<v Ira Glass>嗯，我们的节目今天由娜迪亚·雷曼制作，南希·厄普代克编辑。今天节目组的人员包括查娜·乔夫-沃尔特、阿德里恩·莉莉、托宾·洛、莫莉·马塞洛、凯瑟琳·雷·蒙多、斯托·纳尔逊、鲁西·佩蒂托、瑞安·鲁梅里、鲁比·施瓦茨、弗朗西斯·斯旺森、克里斯托弗·斯威塔拉、玛丽莎·罗伯逊-泰克斯特、朱莉·惠特克和黛安·吴。我们的主编是莎拉·阿卜杜拉赫曼，高级编辑，大卫·克斯滕鲍姆。我们的执行编辑是伊曼纽尔·贝里。

00:56:27.710 --> 00:56:50.030
<v Ira Glass>Special thanks today to Emmanuelle Pare, David Borenstein, Katia Kize, Bela Shayevich, Daniel Plyam, Katherine Emery, Ruth and Mark Fitzpatrick, Henry Frank, Chris Lavoie, and Brett Morse. Elaine Appleton Grant's story in Act One was supported by a grant from the National Institute for Health Care Management. This American Life is delivered to public radio stations by PRX, the Public Radio Exchange.
<v Ira Glass>今天特别感谢艾曼纽·帕雷、大卫·博伦斯坦、卡蒂亚·基泽、贝拉·沙耶维奇、丹尼尔·普利亚姆、凯瑟琳·埃默里、鲁思和马克·菲茨帕特里克、亨利·弗兰克、克里斯·拉沃伊和布雷特·莫尔斯。伊莱恩·阿普尔顿·格兰特在第一幕的故事得到了国家医疗保健管理研究所的资助。这部《美国生活》由公共广播交易所PRX发送到公共广播电台。

00:56:50.030 --> 00:57:20.400
<v Ira Glass>Special thanks today to This American Life Partners Sean Collins and Stonewall and Traveler Peek. Sean Collins, I know you. We worked together at NPR. Nice to see you here in this ad. How's Oklahoma? They and all of our Life Partners have subscribed to help us keep making our program. When you do that, you get to listen ad free. And you get other stuff, including bonus episodes. Our most recent one that I did with one of my coworkers, Diane Wu, turned out so much more emotional and personal than I think either of us expected.
